### Files in C – A Quick Primer

In C, a *file* is an abstraction that represents a stream of bytes stored outside the program’s memory (on disk, a terminal, a pipe, etc.). The C Standard Library gives you two complementary ways to work with files:

| Level | API | Typical use‑case |
|-------|-----|------------------|
| **High‑level** | `FILE *` + `fopen`, `fclose`, `fprintf`, `fscanf`, `fread`, `fwrite`, `fseek`, `ftell`, … | Portable, buffered I/O; works with text and binary data. |
| **Low‑level** | POSIX file descriptors (`int fd`) + `open`, `read`, `write`, `lseek`, `close`, … | Unbuffered, more control; needed for non‑blocking I/O, sockets, etc. |

Below we’ll focus on the high‑level `FILE *` interface because it’s the most common in everyday C programs.

---

## 1. Opening a File

```c
#include <stdio.h>

FILE *fp = fopen("example.txt", "r");   // read‑only, text mode
if (!fp) {
    perror("fopen failed");
    return 1;
}
```

| Mode string | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| `"r"` | Read text (fails if file doesn’t exist). |
| `"w"` | Write text (creates/truncates). |
| `"a"` | Append text (creates if missing). |
| `"rb"`, `"wb"`, `"ab"` | Same as above but binary mode (no translation). |
| `"r+"`, `"w+"`, `"a+"` | Read/write variants. |
| `"x"` | Create new file only (fails if exists). |

> **Tip:** On Windows, `"b"` matters because text mode translates `\n` ↔ `\r\n`. On Unix/Linux, it’s harmless but still required for portability.

---

## 2. Reading & Writing

### Text I/O

```c
char line[256];
while (fgets(line, sizeof line, fp)) {   // reads until newline or EOF
    printf("%s", line);                  // prints exactly what was read
}
```

- `fgets` keeps the terminating `'\0'`.
- Use `fputs` or `fprintf` to write strings.

### Binary I/O

```c
unsigned char buffer[1024];
size_t n = fread(buffer, 1, sizeof buffer, fp);   // reads up to 1024 bytes
// process buffer[0..n-1]
```

- `fread` returns the number of *objects* successfully read (here, bytes).
- `fwrite` works similarly.

---

## 3. Seeking & Positioning

```c
fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_END);          // move to end
long size = ftell(fp);           // get current position (file size)
rewind(fp);                      // same as fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_SET);
```

- `ftell` returns a `long`; for very large files use `ftello`/`fseeko` with `off_t`.

---

## 4. Closing

```c
if (fclose(fp) != 0) {
    perror("fclose failed");
}
```

Always close to flush buffers and release resources.

---

## 5. Error Handling

- `ferror(fp)` tells you if an error flag is set.
- `feof(fp)` tells you if EOF has been reached.
- After `fread`/`fwrite`, check the return value against the requested count.

---

## 6. Low‑Level Example (POSIX)

```c
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int fd = open("example.bin", O_RDONLY);
if (fd == -1) { perror("open"); exit(1); }

char buf[512];
ssize_t n = read(fd, buf, sizeof buf);
if (n == -1) { perror("read"); }

close(fd);
```

- No buffering; you manage your own buffers.
- Works on Unix-like systems; Windows has `_open`, `_read`, etc.

---

## 7. Common Pitfalls

| Problem | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| Forgetting to close a file | Always call `fclose` (or `close`). |
| Mixing `fread`/`fwrite` with `printf`/`scanf` on the same `FILE *` without flushing | Call `fflush(fp)` before switching between text and binary modes. |
| Using `fseek`/`ftell` on a stream opened in text mode | Only safe on binary streams; otherwise results are undefined. |
| Assuming `fread` returns the exact number of bytes requested | Check the return value; loop until you’ve read all needed data. |

---

## 8. Quick Reference Code

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    FILE *fp = fopen("data.bin", "rb");
    if (!fp) { perror("fopen"); return EXIT_FAILURE; }

    unsigned char buf[128];
    size_t total = 0;
    while (1) {
        size_t n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, fp);
        if (n == 0) break;                 // EOF or error
        total += n;
        // process buf[0..n-1] here
    }
    if (ferror(fp)) perror("fread");

    if (fclose(fp) != 0) perror("fclose");
    printf("Read %zu bytes.\n", total);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

---

## 9. Further Reading

- **C99 Standard §7.19.5** – File I/O functions.
- **POSIX.1‑2017** – Low‑level file descriptor API.
- “The C Programming Language” (K&R) – Chapter 5, “Input and Output”.
- Online tutorials: [cppreference.com](https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/io), [tutorialspoint.com](https://www.tutorialspoint.com/c_standard_library/c_file_io.htm).

---

### TL;DR

- **Files** are handled via `FILE *` (high‑level) or file descriptors (low‑level).
- Use `fopen`/`fclose` to open/close.
- Read/write with `fread`/`fwrite` (binary) or `fgets`/`fprintf` (text).
- Handle errors with `ferror`, `feof`, and return values.
- Remember to flush (`fflush`) when mixing text/binary or switching modes.

Happy coding!
